# Done-gate

**This file is yours.** `rasa.module.tasks` ships the portable lifecycle in
`.claude/task-rules.md`, but it deliberately does not decide what *done*
means here -- that is the one thing that varies between every project that
mounts it. This is where you decide.

## Where the gate runs

A task in `review/` has had its work finished. It moves to `completed/` only
when **both** of these hold:

1. every acceptance criterion on the task is met and actually verified, and
2. **every gate below passes.**

If a gate does not pass, the task goes back to `active/` with the failing
gate named. It does not sit in `review/` waiting to be forgiven, and it does
not proceed with a gate waived. A gate you are willing to step around is not
a gate -- delete it from this file and be honest about the bar instead.

Each gate must be **objectively checkable**: something that plainly succeeds
or fails, a named person's approval, or a document that now exists. Never a
feeling, and never "looks fine".

## Where the evidence goes

Whatever satisfied the gate is recorded in the task's `## Notes` section --
what was checked, what was observed, who approved, when. The command that
moves the task also writes one line to `tasks/history.tsv`, and its `note`
column is where you name the gate: `gate: second read 2027-03-13, no
findings`. Between the two, a reader in a year can tell what "done" actually
meant on the day.

---

## Gates

> **Edit this section.** Until you do, the default below applies. It is the
> most honest bar that one person working alone can genuinely satisfy: it
> invents nobody, and no item on it is passed by feeling finished. Running on
> the default is a reasonable start and a poor destination.

- [ ] **Criteria met** -- every acceptance criterion on the task is checked,
      and each one names in `## Notes` what was observed and where.
- [ ] **Checked from cold** -- the result was verified from a clean start,
      not from the state you happened to already be in.
- [ ] **Slept on** -- at least one clear break between finishing the work and
      passing this gate, and the work re-read against the acceptance criteria
      after that break. Record the interval.
- [ ] **Nothing else touched** -- nothing outside "Artifacts expected to
      change" was altered, or the task was amended first and says why.
- [ ] **Closing note written** -- `## Notes` says what was done, what was
      verified and how, and what was deliberately left undone.

---

## Examples

Pick the block nearest your project, paste its gates above the line, and
tune them. These are illustrations, not a menu of approved answers.

### A second person is available

The strongest gate there is, when there is genuinely somebody else. Do not
adopt it if that person would be you wearing a different hat.

```markdown
- [ ] Acceptance criteria met and individually verified.
- [ ] A second person, who did not do the work, has examined it and approved.
- [ ] Their approval is recorded on the task: who, when, and what they looked at.
```

### A practice

```markdown
- [ ] Conflicts cleared for the matter.
- [ ] Every authority cited has been checked against the source.
- [ ] Privilege and confidentiality handling confirmed for everything produced.
- [ ] Supervising practitioner has signed off.
- [ ] Every filing or service deadline the work touches has been diarised.
```

### A clinic

```markdown
- [ ] The affected pathway validates against the current clinical rules.
- [ ] Handling of identifiable patient material reviewed: none leaked into
      the ledger, and the access record is intact.
- [ ] Regulatory sign-off recorded where the change requires one.
- [ ] A clinical reviewer has approved.
```

### A manuscript

```markdown
- [ ] Continuity checked against the bible: people, timeline, established fact.
- [ ] Voice pass against the project's own voice notes.
- [ ] Read aloud once, start to finish, without stopping to fix anything.
- [ ] Editorial approval, or -- working alone -- a re-read after a full day away.
```

### Work that a machine can check

If some part of your work can be checked mechanically, say so precisely: the
exact command, and what its success means. Name it here rather than in the
task, so that every task inherits the same bar.

```markdown
- [ ] `<the project's own verification command>` succeeds, with no new warnings.
- [ ] The check was run from a clean starting state, not an incremental one.
- [ ] Its output is recorded in the task's `## Notes`.
```

---

## Two things this file is not

**It is not a place for the lifecycle rules.** How tasks move, what the
directories mean, what the frontmatter must contain -- all of that is in
`.claude/task-rules.md` and is the same everywhere. Only the definition of
*verified* belongs here.

**It is not the place for your project's working conventions at large.** If
your project needs a broader set of house rules on top of the portable
lifecycle, put them in `.claude/<project>-task-rules.md` and keep this file
to the gate itself. A done-gate that has grown into a style guide stops being
read at the moment it matters.
